# Unpin left the session with no title: the lost update between a generated title and the blank

Date: 2026-10-03. Host: Linux, no Windows or macOS involved. Crate: `xai-grok-shell`.
Row closed: `TODO.md`, "GeneratedTitle 与取消置顶之间的盘级 lost-update 仍靠调用方各自兜".

== 1. Two writers, no shared order

An auto title and an unpin both write the session title, and they do it from different places:

- The title. `SummaryGenerator::update` (`session/summary.rs`) spawns a task on the first content
  chunk; that task calls the sampler and sends `PersistenceMsg::GeneratedTitle` back. The actor writes
  it with `set_generated_title_if_absent`, which refuses when a title is already present. The refusal is
  the point: a manual `/rename` must never be clobbered by a slower LLM title.
- The unpin. `extensions/session_admin.rs::reset_session_title_to_auto` calls
  `storage.reset_title_to_auto(&info)` itself, blanking `generated_title` and `session_summary` so that
  `display_title()` is empty, and only afterwards enqueues `PersistenceMsg::ResetTitleToAuto` (which
  resets the generator and clears the remote cache).

Nothing orders those two writes. The blank is a direct storage call from the extension; the title is a
message in the actor's queue. Both interleavings are reachable:

      blank lands first    GeneratedTitle arrives against a blank title -> adopted. Fine.
      title lands first    GeneratedTitle arrives while the pin is still on disk -> refused,
                           then the blank lands, and nothing offers that title again.

The second row is the defect. From the outside the symptom is a session that was unpinned and stayed
untitled until some later turn regenerated a title.

== 2. What was holding the invariant up before

Only the caller. `reset_session_title_to_auto` compensates in two branches: a resident session gets
`SessionCommand::TitleRenamed { manual: false }`, a dormant one gets `title_refresh_idx` written back to
0. Both reopen the whole-conversation refresh, so a title eventually arrives. `SummaryGenerator::reset`
returns the generator to `Idle` so a later `ContentChunk` can retry.

That is discipline, not a guarantee. It also has a cost that is easy to miss: the refresh has to wait
for the next prompt, so the blank window is a property of the product, not of the race.

== 3. The fix

The actor already sees both facts, the rejection and the reset, in its own queue. So it holds the
refused title and replays it at the reset (`session/persistence.rs`):

      PersistenceMsg::GeneratedTitle(title) => ...
          Ok(false) => {
              // Held for the unpin path, which is the only thing that can order
              // this rejection against the write that blanks the title.
              self.pending_auto_title = Some(title);

      PersistenceMsg::ResetTitleToAuto => {
          self.summary.reset();
          if let Some(sync) = &self.remote_sync {
              sync.clear_title();
          }
          self.replay_held_auto_title().await;
      }

`replay_held_auto_title` takes the held title (so one rejection is replayed at most once), offers it
through the same `set_generated_title_if_absent`, and announces it through the same
`announce_adopted_title` an adoption uses, so the client, the remote cache and the registry see it
exactly as they would see any adopted auto title. `Ok(false)` there is still possible and still means a
title is present; it is logged at debug, not written.

There is one writer of the field and one consumer of it. The adoption arms do not clear it, because
clearing there is unreachable: a rejection can only be followed by a successful if-absent write after a
blank, and the blank is the reset, which already consumed the hold.

== 4. Why this is not the freeze that the row was afraid of

The earlier row declined to accept late auto titles on the grounds that it would pin the title at the
first turn. That objection holds for an unconditional accept; it does not hold here, and it was checked
rather than argued.

The same unpin that triggers the replay also resets the refresh watermark:
`on_title_renamed(false)` sets `next_title_refresh_idx` to 0 and persists it
(`acp_session_impl/title_refresh.rs`). The next prompt therefore runs the whole-conversation refresh,
which sends `PersistenceMsg::RegenerateTitle` (line 114 of the same file). `RegenerateTitle` goes to
`regenerate_generated_title`, which overwrites an auto title and is not gated on the title being empty.
The replayed title is provisional by construction, and
`replayed_held_title_is_overwritten_by_the_refresh_it_reopens` asserts exactly that on the shipped
actor: replay the held title, then send `RegenerateTitle`, then read `summary.json` and require the
refreshed title on disk with `title_is_manual == false`.

== 5. The four tests, and which mutation each one catches

All four are in `session::persistence::durable_update_tests` and drive the shipped actor through the
messages production sends. `GeneratedTitle` is sent directly rather than produced by a live sampler
because the unpin's blank does not go through the actor at all: the actor's own queue is the only place
where the two orderings can be forced, and forcing them is the difference between testing the ordering
and testing the scheduler.

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib session::persistence
    test result: ok. 140 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 6752 filtered out

      test                                            caught
      unpin_replays_the_auto_title_that_lost_the_race MG, MH
      rejected_auto_title_is_not_adopted_without_an_unpin  MH
      replayed_held_title_is_overwritten_by_the_refresh_it_reopens  MG
      a_spent_held_title_is_not_replayed_by_a_later_unpin  MG, MI

Every test catches at least one mutation, which is the check that none of them is a description of the
code rather than a constraint on it.

== 6. The mutation batch

      MG drop `self.replay_held_auto_title().await;` from the reset handler
         RED, 3 failed: the replay test, the refresh test, the spent-hold test
      MH write the refused title at rejection time (`update_session_title`) instead of holding it
         RED, 2 failed: rejected_auto_title_is_not_adopted_without_an_unpin and the replay test
      MI replay with `clone()` instead of `take()`, so the hold is never spent
         RED, 1 failed: a_spent_held_title_is_not_replayed_by_a_later_unpin

MH is the one that decides whether `rejected_auto_title_is_not_adopted_without_an_unpin` is a guard or a
comment: it is the only test besides the replay test that notices an auto title overwriting a manual
pin. MI is caught by exactly one test, which is why the spent-hold test exists.

    baseline: exit=0, 42 passed; 0 failed
    restored byte-identically: True
    final run after restore: exit=0, 42 passed; 0 failed

Each mutation is applied by writing the file and reverted by copying the saved pristine bytes back, and
the last comparison is a byte comparison rather than a re-read of the file.

== 7. Boundaries of what was closed

- A dormant session has no actor, so nothing can hold a title for it. It also has no in-flight
  generation for the same reason, so there is no lost update to lose; its title comes from the
  `title_refresh_idx = 0` watermark on the next resume. This change does not reach that path and does not
  claim to.
- The replay can only run where a `PersistenceMsg` can be delivered. `reset_session_title_to_auto`
  enqueues it when `resident_handle` returns a handle, which requires a hosted presence
  (`agent/mvp_agent/session_registry.rs:353`), and a hosted presence is the same condition under which a
  hold can exist at all. The dormant branch therefore has no hold to replay, not a hold it forgot about.
- There is still no metric or warning for "an unpin finished with no title available". A `warn` on the
  empty-hold branch would fire on the ordinary unpin, where no title raced and the refresh will supply
  the title, so it would be noise rather than a signal. The invariant now lives in the test suite.
- The write that blanks the title still does not go through the actor. Moving it would be the larger
  change (every title write routed through `PersistenceMsg`), and the replay makes the ordering
  unnecessary rather than making it impossible.
